<p><strong>Mirantte News</strong> was a products-and-services guide that went beyond a regional search mechanism. Selected companies signed annual plans combining homepage prominence, indexing in one of roughly twenty portal categories and a dedicated hot-site with professional photography, logo, contact information, an article produced by journalist <strong>Tatiana Cavalcanti</strong>, and geolocation and route-planning features developed through a partnership with <strong>MapLink/UOL</strong>.</p><p>Marcelo Nicchio ran the project in partnership with <strong>Luiz Kechichian</strong>, owner of Grupo Mirantte. The operation was lean: besides Nicchio, the team came to include a webmaster, a commercial contact and an administrative assistant.</p><p>As contracts accumulated, however, a central problem emerged: <strong>traffic</strong>. The portal's organic volume was not enough to feed all categories evenly and deliver the exposure advertisers expected.</p><p>In that context, a MapLink contact arranged a Skype conversation between Nicchio and <strong>Tiago Luz</strong>, one of the early Search Marketing professionals in Brazil, then based in Curitiba. The solution discussed was straightforward: buy traffic for the categories with the greatest concentration of advertisers and incorporate that cost into the portal's own commercial model.</p><p>The first acquisition campaigns worked and remained active for several months. Even so, Mirantte News became progressively difficult to sustain economically. The operation was reduced and moved to a home-office model, leaving Nicchio and webmaster <strong>Renato Carriel</strong>. Later, the guide shifted its positioning and expanded its scope to the city of São Paulo under the name <strong>Procure SP</strong>, but eventually ceased operations.</p><p>The project ended, but the problem it had presented opened a new professional direction. <strong>Search remained.</strong></p><!-- gallery:mirantte-news:start -->
